[l_english.STATE_LORE_539]
In the western plains of Equestria, the austere black spires of the EEA Tribunal building pierce the sky, as if to puncture all inconvenient fantasies. Each month, several train cars laden with suspects depart from Canterlot, traversing the Whitetail Woods to reach the Tribunal’s domain. However, most of the truly heinous offenders are already dealt with before even reaching Canterlot; those sent here are largely the vaguely “heretical”—hovering just outside the Empress’s definition of Harmonism—caught in a bureaucratic limbo. They might be accused of privately spreading “unofficial friendship theories”, or of having the corners of their mouths curve up while reading “unapproved historical texts”. The Ministry of Order, the Ministry of Defense, or even the Empress herself, having little interest in them, often dispatch them to the western suburbs in the name of “Imperial magnanimity”, simply to keep the palace carpets clean. Only occasionally does Canterlot slip in a few prominent rebels destined to be made public examples, handing them over to the Tribunal.
Typically, perhaps due to the Empress’s uncanny foresight, the Tribunal’s “independent judgments” on major cases align remarkably with Her Majesty’s predictions. However, for those inexplicably unlucky “heretics”, the very laws drafted by the Empress herself often become their salvation. The statute reads: “Anypony who mocks, slanders and disregards the Empress shall be put to death.” Chancellor Neighsay, who can recite every clause and discern every nuance of the law, could not possibly fail to read the Empress’s profound intent between the lines: it must be due to Her Majesty’s kindness, definitely not a forgotten comma during her drafting, that the original three parallel offenses of “mock, slander, and disregard” transform into a single condition requiring both slander and disregard to be simultaneously committed for the law to take effect. Clearly, if some pony goes to such elaborate lengths to slander the Empress, they must hold her in sufficient regard, thus negating the charge of disregard. And since isolated acts of slander or disrespect find no corresponding statute, the accused must be acquitted.
So, when Chancellor Neighsay proclaims the EEA Tribunal as the sole supreme interpreter of Harmonism, you’d best offer him a sincere toast.

[l_simp_chinese.STATE_LORE_539]
在小马利亚的西部平原上，教协法院大楼那庄严的黑色尖顶直插云霄，仿佛要刺破所有不合时宜的幻想。每月，都有几车满载着嫌犯的列车从坎特洛特出发，穿过白尾森林，抵达教协的地盘。只不过，多数十恶不赦者早已在到达坎特洛特前就被执行了判决，送来这里的大多是游离在女皇所定义的谐律主义边缘，不上不下的那一群——他们或是被指控在私下传播“非官方友谊理论”，或是因为在阅读“未经批准的历史文献”时被发现嘴角上扬。秩序部、国防部抑或是女皇都对他们没什么兴趣，便常常以“女皇宽容大度”为名把他们遣送西郊，以免弄脏了皇宫的地毯。只是偶尔，坎特洛特也会塞进去几个要被当做典型昭告天下的叛军，交给教协处理。
通常，或许是因为女皇的明察秋毫吧，教协在重大案件上的“独立审判”会与女皇陛下的预测出奇一致。但对于那些莫名其妙遭了霉运的“异端分子”，女皇亲笔撰写的法条则常常成为他们的救命稻草：“任何嘲笑、污蔑与轻视女皇者，须除以死刑。” 能够熟练背诵法条洞察一切的会长驹绝，不可能不从字里行间读出女皇的深刻用意：一定是因为女皇的善良，而不是在“亲笔撰写”时抄漏了一个逗号，才使得原本“嘲笑、污蔑、与轻视女皇”的三种并列行为变成了必须同时“既污蔑而又轻视”才可生效的法条——当你费尽心机去污蔑女皇的时候，显然对女皇足够的重视，因此罪名不能成立。而由于单独的污蔑与轻视都找不到对应的法条，就只得无罪释放。
所以，当会长驹绝将教协法庭称为掌握谐律主义唯一最高解释权的机构时，你最好为他献上真挚的祝酒词。
